clearcote(#12): portable profile encryption key.

Cookie values are encrypted with a key the OS holds -- DPAPI on Windows, Keychain on macOS -- so a
copied profile arrives signed out. Everything else in a user data directory already moves by copying
the folder; cookies did not.

Chromium 151 resolves the key through pluggable KeyProviders registered with a precedence. Only the
highest-precedence provider performs encryption, but EVERY registered provider can still decrypt, so
adding a portable provider above the platform ones gives portability AND free migration: an existing
profile keeps opening, and moves to the portable key as it is written. No export step, no import
step, no new file format.

Two modes, both opt-in; with neither switch the provider is not registered at all and behaviour is
exactly stock:

  --portable-profile              generate a key and store it in the profile
  --profile-encryption-key=<s>    derive the key from a caller secret; nothing is written to disk

A key stored beside the data it protects means the cookie database is effectively unencrypted at
rest. That is inherent to portability rather than a flaw in it, which is why it is opt-in and why
the derived mode is the better default for a profile synced to shared storage.

Notes on the implementation: the provider lives in components/ungoogled rather than in
components/os_crypt so the fork does not edit upstream's BUILD.gn; a corrupt key file is never
overwritten, because silently regenerating one would discard every cookie it protects; and failures
return kTemporarilyUnavailable rather than kPermanentlyUnavailable, since the interface documents
that "permanent" invites callers to drop the data. File IO runs on the thread pool -- GetKey is
callback-shaped for exactly that, and base::ScopedAllowBlocking is friend-gated besides.

Verified on both platforms: a profile copied with its platform-wrapped key removed (simulating a
different machine) loses its cookies without the switch and keeps them with either switch. On Linux
the control keeps them too, because the POSIX provider derives from a hardcoded constant and Linux
profiles were already portable.

--- a/components/ungoogled/portable_key_provider.h
+++ b/components/ungoogled/portable_key_provider.h
